  "id": 21,
  "name": "Lotus Blossom",
  "slug": "lotus-blossom",
  "actor_type": "unknown",
  "affiliation": [
    "unknown"
  ],
  "affiliation_unit": null,
  "affiliation_detail": "Attributed to China based on targeting and tooling. No specific PLA unit, MSS bureau, or contractor has been publicly identified. No indictments. \nSOCRadar (2026) describes the group as occupying \"a specialized niche\" within the Chinese APT ecosystem focused on long-term intelligence collection \"integrated with national strategic planning.\"\n",
  "affiliation_confidence": "suspected",
  "entity_name": null,
  "last_seen": "2026",
  "first_seen": "2009",
  "first_described": "2015-06",
  "description": "Lotus Blossom is a suspected Chinese state-aligned espionage group active since at least 2009, targeting government, military, diplomatic, telecom, and critical infrastructure organizations primarily in Southeast Asia. The group is defined by patient, long-dwell-time intelligence collection and a distinctive custom malware. (SOCRadar, 2026)\nIn 2015, Palo Alto's Unit 42 attributed a targeted attack directed at an French diplomat based in Taipei  to Lotus Blossom, noting likely interest in \"gaining insights into relations between France and Taiwan.\"\nThe group progressed from spear-phishing with weaponized Office documents to living-off-the-land techniques and supply chain attacks. (SOCRadar, 2026) In 2022, Symantec documented Lotus Blossom (as Billbug) targeting a national Certificate Authority alongside government agencies in multiple Asian countries. \nIn 2025, the group executed a supply chain compromise of Notepad++ update infrastructure to deliver the Chrysalis backdoor to selectively targeted in Southeast Asia across government, telecommunications and critical infrastructure sectors over six months. (Rapid7, 2026; Palo Alto Unit 42, 2026)\n",
  "attack_group_id": "G0030",
  "attack_group_url": "https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0030/",
